{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"id\":\"U-سمو\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"Across Quranic uses, بِاسْم can frame an action: reading in 96:1 and boarding in 11:41; 22:36 places Allah’s name over a ritual act. Since the Fatiha is recited in salah and opens with بِسْمِ, its compressed opening can be heard as framing the recitation itself under Allah’s name.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:1\",\"ar\":\"بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَٰنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ\"},{\"ref\":\"96:1\",\"ar\":\"ٱقْرَأْ بِٱسْمِ رَبِّكَ ٱلَّذِى خَلَقَ\"},{\"ref\":\"11:41\",\"ar\":\"بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ مَجْر۪ىٰهَا وَمُرْسَىٰهَآ\"},{\"ref\":\"22:36\",\"ar\":\"فَٱذْكُرُوا۟ ٱسْمَ ٱللَّهِ عَلَيْهَا صَوَآفَّ\"}],\"activation\":\"The opening بِسْمِ and the Fatiha’s recitation in every salah make an unspoken act of reciting a live possibility.\",\"limits\":\"These parallels show the name framing different acts; they do not determine the exact omitted governing act in 1:1.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"U-رحم\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The ٱلرَّحْمَٰنِ announced in 1:1 returns in 1:3 immediately before مَٰلِكِ يَوْمِ ٱلدِّينِ in 1:4. Since another Quranic use places ٱلرَّحْمَٰنُ over the Throne, the title can carry from the opening mercy into the Fatiha’s sovereignty and judgment sequence.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:1\",\"ar\":\"بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَٰنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:3\",\"ar\":\"ٱلرَّحْمَٰنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:4\",\"ar\":\"مَٰلِكِ يَوْمِ ٱلدِّينِ\"},{\"ref\":\"20:5\",\"ar\":\"ٱلرَّحْمَٰنُ عَلَى ٱلْعَرْشِ ٱسْتَوَىٰ\"}],\"activation\":\"The repeated title at 1:3 directly precedes the ownership of the Day of Judgment at 1:4.\",\"limits\":\"The sequence frames judgment with the mercy title, but does not explicitly equate مَٰلِكِ with ٱلرَّحْمَٰنِ or explain their relation.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"X1\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The expanded basmala in 1:1 also appears after إِنَّهُۥ مِن سُلَيْمَٰنَ in 27:30. In the Fatiha it opens a recitation that turns into direct address at 1:5; the shared formula can mark the threshold of a communicative act in both settings.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:1\",\"ar\":\"بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَٰنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ\"},{\"ref\":\"27:30\",\"ar\":\"إِنَّهُۥ مِن سُلَيْمَٰنَ وَإِنَّهُۥ بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَٰنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:5\",\"ar\":\"إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ وَإِيَّاكَ نَسْتَعِينُ\"}],\"activation\":\"The exact expanded formula recurs at 27:30, while the Fatiha proceeds from its opening into direct address to God.\",\"limits\":\"The matching formula does not establish that the two speech settings have the same purpose or audience.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}"}}
